1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,869
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:02,049 --> 00:00:03,674
How much money do you need to

3
00:00:03,674 --> 00:00:04,626
never work again?

4
00:00:04,806 --> 00:00:06,571
Most people have no idea.

5
00:00:06,751 --> 00:00:09,064
They guess a number like one million

6
00:00:09,064 --> 00:00:11,441
or five million and hope it's enough.

7
00:00:11,621 --> 00:00:13,543
But there's a simple rule of thumb

8
00:00:13,543 --> 00:00:15,579
that gives you a real starting point

9
00:00:15,579 --> 00:00:16,823
in about five seconds.

10
00:00:17,003 --> 00:00:18,941
It's called the four percent rule.

11
00:00:19,121 --> 00:00:21,199
Today I'll show you how it works,

12
00:00:21,199 --> 00:00:22,396
where it came from,

13
00:00:22,396 --> 00:00:23,403
where it breaks,

14
00:00:23,403 --> 00:00:25,041
and how to use it to build

15
00:00:25,041 --> 00:00:26,552
your own freedom number.

16
00:00:26,901 --> 00:00:27,772
Here's the rule.

17
00:00:27,952 --> 00:00:29,731
Take what you spend in a year

18
00:00:29,731 --> 00:00:31,633
and multiply it by twenty five.

19
00:00:31,813 --> 00:00:34,104
If you spend forty thousand dollars a

20
00:00:34,104 --> 00:00:36,642
year, your target is one million dollars.

21
00:00:36,822 --> 00:00:38,041
Why twenty five?

22
00:00:38,221 --> 00:00:40,719
Because four percent of one million is

23
00:00:40,719 --> 00:00:41,704
forty thousand.

24
00:00:41,884 --> 00:00:43,981
The idea is that you could withdraw

25
00:00:43,981 --> 00:00:46,196
about four percent in your first year

26
00:00:46,196 --> 00:00:49,011
of retirement, adjust for inflation after that,

27
00:00:49,011 --> 00:00:50,808
and have a good chance of your

28
00:00:50,808 --> 00:00:52,844
money lasting around thirty years.

29
00:00:53,195 --> 00:00:56,216
That simple multiplication changes how people think

30
00:00:56,216 --> 00:00:57,223
about retirement.

31
00:00:57,403 --> 00:01:00,108
Suddenly, freedom isn't a vague dream.

32
00:01:00,288 --> 00:01:02,275
It's a number tied directly to your

33
00:01:02,275 --> 00:01:02,842
lifestyle.

34
00:01:03,022 --> 00:01:05,066
And it reveals something powerful.

35
00:01:05,246 --> 00:01:07,753
Lowering your spending doesn't just help you

36
00:01:07,753 --> 00:01:08,322
save more.

37
00:01:08,502 --> 00:01:10,581
It also lowers the number you need.

38
00:01:10,761 --> 00:01:13,037
Every one thousand dollars you cut from

39
00:01:13,037 --> 00:01:15,605
yearly spending reduces your target by about

40
00:01:15,605 --> 00:01:17,297
twenty five thousand dollars.

41
00:01:17,648 --> 00:01:19,733
We'll look at the four percent rule

42
00:01:19,733 --> 00:01:20,805
from three angles.

43
00:01:20,985 --> 00:01:22,425
First, the numbers.

44
00:01:22,605 --> 00:01:24,165
Where the rule came from,

45
00:01:24,165 --> 00:01:26,161
what it assumes and how risky it

46
00:01:26,161 --> 00:01:26,785
really is.

47
00:01:26,965 --> 00:01:28,892
Second, the psychology.

48
00:01:29,072 --> 00:01:31,424
Why spending your savings is so hard,

49
00:01:31,424 --> 00:01:33,331
and why so many people get the

50
00:01:33,331 --> 00:01:34,157
number wrong.

51
00:01:34,337 --> 00:01:35,858
And third, the playbook.

52
00:01:36,038 --> 00:01:37,993
How to calculate your own number,

53
00:01:37,993 --> 00:01:39,533
reach it and make it last.

54
00:01:39,713 --> 00:01:41,561
Reminder, this is education,

55
00:01:41,561 --> 00:01:42,947
not financial advice,

56
00:01:42,947 --> 00:01:45,785
and nothing about the future is guaranteed.

57
00:01:46,135 --> 00:01:47,655
Let's start with the numbers.

58
00:01:47,835 --> 00:01:50,146
And first, where did this rule actually

59
00:01:50,146 --> 00:01:50,738
come from?

60
00:01:51,088 --> 00:01:51,877
Our numbers.

61
00:01:52,057 --> 00:01:55,141
The four percent rule comes from William

62
00:01:55,141 --> 00:01:57,993
Bengen's 1994 study in the Journal of

63
00:01:57,993 --> 00:02:01,385
Financial Planning, and was supported by the

64
00:02:01,385 --> 00:02:04,160
1998 Trinity study by Philip Cooley,

65
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:06,395
Carl Hubbard and Daniel Walz.

66
00:02:06,575 --> 00:02:10,093
Recent safe withdrawal research comes from Morningstar.

67
00:02:10,273 --> 00:02:12,985
Social Security figures come from the Social

68
00:02:12,985 --> 00:02:14,464
Security Administration.

69
00:02:14,644 --> 00:02:17,788
Growth examples use stated average returns,

70
00:02:17,788 --> 00:02:20,055
as illustrations, not promises.

71
00:02:20,405 --> 00:02:21,101
Angle one.

72
00:02:21,281 --> 00:02:21,931
The numbers.

73
00:02:22,111 --> 00:02:24,363
Seven lessons about what the four percent

74
00:02:24,363 --> 00:02:25,297
rule really says,

75
00:02:25,297 --> 00:02:26,395
and what it doesn't.

76
00:02:26,746 --> 00:02:27,988
Lesson number one.

77
00:02:28,168 --> 00:02:30,327
Where the four percent rule came from.

78
00:02:30,678 --> 00:02:34,218
In 1994, a financial planner named William

79
00:02:34,218 --> 00:02:36,831
Bengen asked a simple question.

80
00:02:37,011 --> 00:02:39,080
How much can a retiree withdraw from

81
00:02:39,080 --> 00:02:41,380
savings each year without running out of

82
00:02:41,380 --> 00:02:41,724
money?

83
00:02:41,904 --> 00:02:43,646
He tested historical U.

84
00:02:43,826 --> 00:02:44,151
S.

85
00:02:44,331 --> 00:02:46,956
market data going back to 1926,

86
00:02:46,956 --> 00:02:51,274
imagining retirees starting in every possible year.

87
00:02:51,624 --> 00:02:54,576
He assumed a portfolio split between stocks

88
00:02:54,576 --> 00:02:57,254
and intermediate term government bonds.

89
00:02:57,434 --> 00:02:59,944
The retiree withdrew a set percentage in

90
00:02:59,944 --> 00:03:02,578
year one, then increased the dollar amount

91
00:03:02,578 --> 00:03:04,146
each year with inflation,

92
00:03:04,146 --> 00:03:05,840
no matter what markets did.

93
00:03:06,190 --> 00:03:08,435
His finding was that a first year

94
00:03:08,435 --> 00:03:10,680
withdrawal of about four percent,

95
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:12,925
adjusted for inflation afterward,

96
00:03:12,925 --> 00:03:15,442
lasted at least thirty years in every

97
00:03:15,442 --> 00:03:17,347
historical period he tested,

98
00:03:17,347 --> 00:03:20,612
including retirements that began just before the

99
00:03:20,612 --> 00:03:23,470
Great Depression and the high inflation of

100
00:03:23,470 --> 00:03:24,150
the 1970s.

101
00:03:24,501 --> 00:03:25,608
Four years later,

102
00:03:25,608 --> 00:03:28,865
three professors at Trinity University published a

103
00:03:28,865 --> 00:03:31,861
similar study, often called the Trinity study.

104
00:03:32,041 --> 00:03:34,839
It also found that withdrawal rates around

105
00:03:34,839 --> 00:03:37,904
four percent had high historical success rates

106
00:03:37,904 --> 00:03:40,502
over thirty years for portfolios with a

107
00:03:40,502 --> 00:03:41,968
large share of stocks.

108
00:03:42,318 --> 00:03:44,917
Bengen himself later suggested that with more

109
00:03:44,917 --> 00:03:47,863
asset classes, slightly higher starting rates might

110
00:03:47,863 --> 00:03:49,307
have worked historically.

111
00:03:49,487 --> 00:03:50,925
But four percent stuck,

112
00:03:50,925 --> 00:03:53,051
because it's simple and memorable.

113
00:03:53,231 --> 00:03:55,287
It became one of the most famous

114
00:03:55,287 --> 00:03:56,958
rules in personal finance.

115
00:03:57,308 --> 00:03:58,561
Lesson number two.

116
00:03:58,741 --> 00:04:00,332
Turn your spending into a target.

117
00:04:00,682 --> 00:04:02,711
The flip side of a four percent

118
00:04:02,711 --> 00:04:05,395
withdrawal is the times twenty five rule.

119
00:04:05,575 --> 00:04:08,159
If four percent of your portfolio covers

120
00:04:08,159 --> 00:04:09,515
one year of spending,

121
00:04:09,515 --> 00:04:11,712
then you need twenty five years of

122
00:04:11,712 --> 00:04:12,681
spending saved.

123
00:04:12,861 --> 00:04:14,558
Spend thirty thousand a year,

124
00:04:14,558 --> 00:04:17,133
you need about seven hundred fifty thousand.

125
00:04:17,313 --> 00:04:18,730
Spend sixty thousand,

126
00:04:18,730 --> 00:04:21,295
you need about one point five million.

127
00:04:21,646 --> 00:04:23,728
Notice that this uses spending,

128
00:04:23,728 --> 00:04:24,467
not income.

129
00:04:24,647 --> 00:04:27,182
Someone earning two hundred thousand dollars but

130
00:04:27,182 --> 00:04:29,664
spending one hundred eighty thousand needs four

131
00:04:29,664 --> 00:04:31,090
and a half million dollars.

132
00:04:31,270 --> 00:04:34,308
Someone earning sixty thousand but spending thirty

133
00:04:34,308 --> 00:04:37,284
thousand needs only seven hundred fifty thousand.

134
00:04:37,464 --> 00:04:39,473
Your lifestyle sets your target.

135
00:04:39,823 --> 00:04:41,965
It's also about spending in retirement,

136
00:04:41,965 --> 00:04:43,503
which may differ from today.

137
00:04:43,683 --> 00:04:45,206
Some costs disappear,

138
00:04:45,206 --> 00:04:48,107
like commuting or a mortgage that's paid

139
00:04:48,107 --> 00:04:48,397
off.

140
00:04:48,577 --> 00:04:49,733
Others may rise,

141
00:04:49,733 --> 00:04:51,758
like health care and travel.

142
00:04:52,108 --> 00:04:53,851
And the money that counts is the

143
00:04:53,851 --> 00:04:55,649
money coming from your portfolio.

144
00:04:55,829 --> 00:04:57,707
Income from Social Security,

145
00:04:57,707 --> 00:05:00,055
a pension or part time work reduces

146
00:05:00,055 --> 00:05:02,068
how much you need to withdraw,

147
00:05:02,068 --> 00:05:04,617
and therefore how much you need saved.

148
00:05:04,968 --> 00:05:05,757
Try this.

149
00:05:05,937 --> 00:05:07,632
Add up your yearly spending,

150
00:05:07,632 --> 00:05:10,841
subtract any guaranteed retirement income you expect,

151
00:05:10,841 --> 00:05:13,263
and multiply what's left by twenty five.

152
00:05:13,443 --> 00:05:15,266
That's your first draft freedom number.

153
00:05:15,616 --> 00:05:16,893
Lesson number three.

154
00:05:17,073 --> 00:05:18,826
The order of returns matters.

155
00:05:19,176 --> 00:05:20,238
When you're saving,

156
00:05:20,238 --> 00:05:21,972
the order of good and bad years

157
00:05:21,972 --> 00:05:24,209
doesn't change your final result much if

158
00:05:24,209 --> 00:05:24,992
you just hold.

159
00:05:25,172 --> 00:05:26,739
But when you're withdrawing,

160
00:05:26,739 --> 00:05:27,970
it matters enormously.

161
00:05:28,150 --> 00:05:31,006
This is called sequence of returns risk.

162
00:05:31,357 --> 00:05:32,599
Here's an illustration.

163
00:05:32,779 --> 00:05:35,353
Two retirees each start with one million

164
00:05:35,353 --> 00:05:38,119
dollars and withdraw forty thousand a year.

165
00:05:38,299 --> 00:05:41,299
Both experience exactly the same twelve yearly

166
00:05:41,299 --> 00:05:43,582
returns, just in a different order.

167
00:05:43,762 --> 00:05:46,038
For one, the two bad years come

168
00:05:46,038 --> 00:05:46,479
first.

169
00:05:46,659 --> 00:05:47,662
For the other,

170
00:05:47,662 --> 00:05:48,737
they come last.

171
00:05:49,087 --> 00:05:50,234
After twelve years,

172
00:05:50,234 --> 00:05:52,349
the retiree who faced the bad years

173
00:05:52,349 --> 00:05:55,128
first has roughly seven hundred forty thousand

174
00:05:55,128 --> 00:05:55,913
dollars left.

175
00:05:56,093 --> 00:05:58,041
The one who faced them last has

176
00:05:58,041 --> 00:05:59,298
roughly one million.

177
00:05:59,478 --> 00:06:02,063
Same average, very different result,

178
00:06:02,063 --> 00:06:05,869
because selling investments to fund withdrawals after

179
00:06:05,869 --> 00:06:07,593
a crash locks in losses.

180
00:06:07,943 --> 00:06:09,733
This is why the first five to

181
00:06:09,733 --> 00:06:12,202
ten years of retirement are often called

182
00:06:12,202 --> 00:06:13,190
the danger zone.

183
00:06:13,370 --> 00:06:15,280
A big crash early on can do

184
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:18,179
lasting damage to a portfolio that's also

185
00:06:18,179 --> 00:06:19,593
funding withdrawals.

186
00:06:19,944 --> 00:06:22,573
Many retirees manage this risk by holding

187
00:06:22,573 --> 00:06:24,625
a few years of spending in bonds

188
00:06:24,625 --> 00:06:26,549
or cash, so they don't have to

189
00:06:26,549 --> 00:06:28,473
sell stocks during a downturn,

190
00:06:28,473 --> 00:06:30,781
and by being willing to cut spending

191
00:06:30,781 --> 00:06:32,192
a little in bad years.

192
00:06:32,542 --> 00:06:33,854
Lesson number four.

193
00:06:34,034 --> 00:06:36,007
Four percent isn't a guarantee.

194
00:06:36,358 --> 00:06:38,755
The four percent rule is based on

195
00:06:38,755 --> 00:06:38,900
U.

196
00:06:39,080 --> 00:06:39,394
S.

197
00:06:39,574 --> 00:06:40,166
history.

198
00:06:40,346 --> 00:06:42,970
It worked in every historical thirty year

199
00:06:42,970 --> 00:06:44,314
period Bengen tested,

200
00:06:44,314 --> 00:06:46,746
but there's no promise the future will

201
00:06:46,746 --> 00:06:47,962
look like the past.

202
00:06:48,142 --> 00:06:50,650
Future returns could be higher or lower.

203
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,719
Some researchers have argued that future returns

204
00:06:53,719 --> 00:06:55,871
may be lower than historical averages,

205
00:06:55,871 --> 00:06:58,024
which would make four percent riskier.

206
00:06:58,204 --> 00:07:00,097
Morningstar's recent research,

207
00:07:00,097 --> 00:07:02,747
for example, has estimated a safe starting

208
00:07:02,747 --> 00:07:05,207
withdrawal rate for new retirees in the

209
00:07:05,207 --> 00:07:06,785
high three percent range,

210
00:07:06,785 --> 00:07:08,362
depending on assumptions.

211
00:07:08,713 --> 00:07:11,354
Others point out that in most historical

212
00:07:11,354 --> 00:07:14,523
periods, a four percent withdrawal left retirees

213
00:07:14,523 --> 00:07:17,032
with much more money than they started

214
00:07:17,032 --> 00:07:17,362
with.

215
00:07:17,542 --> 00:07:19,890
The rule was designed around the worst

216
00:07:19,890 --> 00:07:21,929
cases, so in typical cases it may

217
00:07:21,929 --> 00:07:22,917
be conservative.

218
00:07:23,268 --> 00:07:25,272
The truth is that four percent is

219
00:07:25,272 --> 00:07:26,973
a reasonable starting point,

220
00:07:26,973 --> 00:07:28,248
not a precise answer.

221
00:07:28,428 --> 00:07:30,107
Think of it as a guide for

222
00:07:30,107 --> 00:07:32,109
planning, not a law of physics.

223
00:07:32,459 --> 00:07:33,213
Try this.

224
00:07:33,393 --> 00:07:35,646
Run your numbers at both three and

225
00:07:35,646 --> 00:07:37,236
a half and four percent.

226
00:07:37,416 --> 00:07:39,224
If you'd be comfortable at either,

227
00:07:39,224 --> 00:07:40,713
you have a margin of safety.

228
00:07:41,064 --> 00:07:42,329
Lesson number five.

229
00:07:42,509 --> 00:07:44,715
The rule assumes about thirty years.

230
00:07:45,065 --> 00:07:48,756
Bengen's research focused on retirements lasting around

231
00:07:48,756 --> 00:07:51,977
thirty years, which fits someone retiring around

232
00:07:51,977 --> 00:07:52,716
sixty five.

233
00:07:52,896 --> 00:07:55,029
But someone retiring at forty might need

234
00:07:55,029 --> 00:07:56,843
their money to last fifty years or

235
00:07:56,843 --> 00:07:57,110
more.

236
00:07:57,460 --> 00:08:01,593
Longer retirements generally require lower withdrawal rates.

237
00:08:01,773 --> 00:08:04,478
Many early retirement planners use three to

238
00:08:04,478 --> 00:08:06,553
three and a half percent for very

239
00:08:06,553 --> 00:08:09,573
long horizons, which means saving roughly twenty

240
00:08:09,573 --> 00:08:12,215
nine to thirty three times yearly spending

241
00:08:12,215 --> 00:08:13,661
instead of twenty five.

242
00:08:14,012 --> 00:08:16,032
Life expectancy matters too.

243
00:08:16,212 --> 00:08:18,584
Many people live into their nineties,

244
00:08:18,584 --> 00:08:21,534
especially couples, where at least one partner

245
00:08:21,534 --> 00:08:23,073
often lives a long time.

246
00:08:23,253 --> 00:08:25,353
Planning only to age eighty five could

247
00:08:25,353 --> 00:08:26,237
leave you short.

248
00:08:26,587 --> 00:08:27,806
On the other hand,

249
00:08:27,806 --> 00:08:30,449
someone retiring at seventy may be able

250
00:08:30,449 --> 00:08:32,007
to withdraw a bit more,

251
00:08:32,007 --> 00:08:34,717
because their money doesn't need to last

252
00:08:34,717 --> 00:08:35,259
as long.

253
00:08:35,610 --> 00:08:36,492
Try this.

254
00:08:36,672 --> 00:08:38,921
Decide how many years your money needs

255
00:08:38,921 --> 00:08:41,347
to last, from your planned retirement age

256
00:08:41,347 --> 00:08:42,767
to at least ninety five,

257
00:08:42,767 --> 00:08:45,194
and adjust your withdrawal rate to match.

258
00:08:45,544 --> 00:08:46,995
Lesson number six.

259
00:08:47,175 --> 00:08:49,056
Your investment mix matters.

260
00:08:49,406 --> 00:08:52,207
The four percent rule assumed a meaningful

261
00:08:52,207 --> 00:08:53,274
share of stocks,

262
00:08:53,274 --> 00:08:55,675
often fifty to seventy five percent.

263
00:08:55,855 --> 00:08:58,478
Stocks provide growth that helps your money

264
00:08:58,478 --> 00:09:00,673
keep up with inflation over decades.

265
00:09:01,023 --> 00:09:03,981
Many people assume retirement money should be

266
00:09:03,981 --> 00:09:05,887
entirely in safe investments.

267
00:09:06,067 --> 00:09:08,842
But a portfolio that's too conservative may

268
00:09:08,842 --> 00:09:11,487
not grow fast enough to support inflation

269
00:09:11,487 --> 00:09:13,939
adjusted withdrawals for thirty years.

270
00:09:14,119 --> 00:09:15,653
In Bengen's research,

271
00:09:15,653 --> 00:09:18,647
very low stock allocations had lower safe

272
00:09:18,647 --> 00:09:19,889
withdrawal rates.

273
00:09:20,239 --> 00:09:22,865
Bonds and cash still play an important

274
00:09:22,865 --> 00:09:23,211
role.

275
00:09:23,391 --> 00:09:26,104
They reduce volatility and provide money to

276
00:09:26,104 --> 00:09:28,375
spend during stock market downturns,

277
00:09:28,375 --> 00:09:30,078
helping with sequence risk.

278
00:09:30,428 --> 00:09:32,658
The right mix depends on your age,

279
00:09:32,658 --> 00:09:35,084
other income and comfort with ups and

280
00:09:35,084 --> 00:09:35,478
downs.

281
00:09:35,658 --> 00:09:38,606
Target date funds and balanced funds offer

282
00:09:38,606 --> 00:09:40,290
simple ready made mixes.

283
00:09:40,641 --> 00:09:41,500
Try this.

284
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:43,546
Look at your current mix of stocks

285
00:09:43,546 --> 00:09:45,358
and bonds and ask whether it fits

286
00:09:45,358 --> 00:09:46,126
your timeline.

287
00:09:46,306 --> 00:09:48,358
If you're decades from retirement,

288
00:09:48,358 --> 00:09:50,651
a very conservative mix may be holding

289
00:09:50,651 --> 00:09:51,194
you back.

290
00:09:51,544 --> 00:09:52,798
Lesson number seven.

291
00:09:52,978 --> 00:09:55,218
Don't forget inflation and taxes.

292
00:09:55,569 --> 00:09:58,409
The four percent rule raises your withdrawals

293
00:09:58,409 --> 00:09:59,987
with inflation each year,

294
00:09:59,987 --> 00:10:01,060
which is crucial.

295
00:10:01,240 --> 00:10:02,966
At three percent inflation,

296
00:10:02,966 --> 00:10:05,652
prices roughly double in about twenty four

297
00:10:05,652 --> 00:10:06,035
years.

298
00:10:06,215 --> 00:10:08,738
Forty thousand dollars of spending today could

299
00:10:08,738 --> 00:10:11,370
mean around eighty thousand later in retirement.

300
00:10:11,720 --> 00:10:13,496
Taxes are another factor.

301
00:10:13,676 --> 00:10:16,985
The rule is typically calculated before taxes.

302
00:10:17,165 --> 00:10:20,467
Withdrawals from traditional four oh one ks

303
00:10:20,467 --> 00:10:23,309
and IRAs are usually taxed as income,

304
00:10:23,309 --> 00:10:26,765
while qualified Roth withdrawals are tax free

305
00:10:26,765 --> 00:10:30,067
and taxable accounts may face capital gains

306
00:10:30,067 --> 00:10:30,528
taxes.

307
00:10:30,878 --> 00:10:33,354
So if you need forty thousand dollars

308
00:10:33,354 --> 00:10:34,425
a year to spend,

309
00:10:34,425 --> 00:10:36,701
you may need to withdraw more than

310
00:10:36,701 --> 00:10:39,044
that from pre tax accounts to cover

311
00:10:39,044 --> 00:10:39,446
taxes.

312
00:10:39,626 --> 00:10:41,634
Your freedom number should account for this.

313
00:10:41,985 --> 00:10:45,223
Having money in different types of accounts,

314
00:10:45,223 --> 00:10:47,136
pre tax, Roth and taxable,

315
00:10:47,136 --> 00:10:50,080
can give you flexibility to manage taxes

316
00:10:50,080 --> 00:10:51,110
in retirement.

317
00:10:51,460 --> 00:10:52,272
Try this.

318
00:10:52,452 --> 00:10:55,282
Estimate how much of your retirement savings

319
00:10:55,282 --> 00:10:56,761
is in pre tax accounts,

320
00:10:56,761 --> 00:10:58,883
and add a cushion to your freedom

321
00:10:58,883 --> 00:10:59,976
number for taxes.

322
00:11:00,326 --> 00:11:01,266
That's angle one.

323
00:11:01,446 --> 00:11:03,422
Where the rule came from,

324
00:11:03,422 --> 00:11:05,555
the times twenty five rule,

325
00:11:05,555 --> 00:11:07,846
sequence risk, no guarantees,

326
00:11:07,846 --> 00:11:10,138
time horizon, investment mix,

327
00:11:10,138 --> 00:11:12,034
and inflation and taxes.

328
00:11:12,214 --> 00:11:13,306
Now angle two.

329
00:11:13,486 --> 00:11:14,589
The psychology.

330
00:11:14,939 --> 00:11:17,111
The math of retirement is only half

331
00:11:17,111 --> 00:11:17,980
the challenge.

332
00:11:18,160 --> 00:11:19,786
The other half is emotional.

333
00:11:19,966 --> 00:11:22,511
Saving for decades and then spending your

334
00:11:22,511 --> 00:11:25,180
savings feels very different from what most

335
00:11:25,180 --> 00:11:26,050
people expect.

336
00:11:26,230 --> 00:11:29,209
Here are seven psychological lessons about the

337
00:11:29,209 --> 00:11:30,374
four percent rule.

338
00:11:30,725 --> 00:11:31,886
Lesson number eight.

339
00:11:32,066 --> 00:11:33,927
A number turns a dream into a

340
00:11:33,927 --> 00:11:34,248
plan.

341
00:11:34,599 --> 00:11:37,617
Most people approach retirement with vague hopes.

342
00:11:37,797 --> 00:11:40,363
Someday, enough, comfortable.

343
00:11:40,543 --> 00:11:41,618
Without a number,

344
00:11:41,618 --> 00:11:43,831
it's hard to know whether you're on

345
00:11:43,831 --> 00:11:46,675
track, so many people either worry constantly

346
00:11:46,675 --> 00:11:48,066
or ignore it entirely.

347
00:11:48,417 --> 00:11:50,301
The four percent rule gives you a

348
00:11:50,301 --> 00:11:51,215
concrete target.

349
00:11:51,395 --> 00:11:52,797
Once you know you need,

350
00:11:52,797 --> 00:11:54,931
say, one point two million dollars,

351
00:11:54,931 --> 00:11:57,004
you can calculate how much to save

352
00:11:57,004 --> 00:11:59,139
each month and track your progress.

353
00:11:59,489 --> 00:12:01,206
Research on goal setting,

354
00:12:01,206 --> 00:12:04,436
including work by psychologists Edwin Locke and

355
00:12:04,436 --> 00:12:06,978
Gary Latham, has found that specific,

356
00:12:06,978 --> 00:12:09,726
challenging goals tend to lead to better

357
00:12:09,726 --> 00:12:12,543
performance than vague goals like do your

358
00:12:12,543 --> 00:12:12,887
best.

359
00:12:13,237 --> 00:12:15,492
Tracking progress toward your number can be

360
00:12:15,492 --> 00:12:16,070
motivating.

361
00:12:16,250 --> 00:12:18,907
Watching yourself move from ten percent to

362
00:12:18,907 --> 00:12:21,376
twenty five percent to fifty percent of

363
00:12:21,376 --> 00:12:23,654
the way there makes the journey feel

364
00:12:23,654 --> 00:12:23,970
real.

365
00:12:24,320 --> 00:12:25,225
Try this.

366
00:12:25,405 --> 00:12:27,794
Calculate what percentage of your freedom number

367
00:12:27,794 --> 00:12:28,540
you have today.

368
00:12:28,720 --> 00:12:30,595
Write it down and check it once

369
00:12:30,595 --> 00:12:31,019
a year.

370
00:12:31,369 --> 00:12:32,483
Lesson number nine.

371
00:12:32,663 --> 00:12:35,566
Spending savings is surprisingly hard.

372
00:12:35,916 --> 00:12:37,565
After decades of saving,

373
00:12:37,565 --> 00:12:40,725
many retirees find it emotionally difficult to

374
00:12:40,725 --> 00:12:41,755
start spending.

375
00:12:41,935 --> 00:12:44,262
Watching a balance go down can feel

376
00:12:44,262 --> 00:12:46,920
like failure, even when it's exactly the

377
00:12:46,920 --> 00:12:47,253
plan.

378
00:12:47,603 --> 00:12:50,745
Research by the Employee Benefit Research Institute

379
00:12:50,745 --> 00:12:53,147
and others has found that many retirees

380
00:12:53,147 --> 00:12:55,119
spend down their savings slowly,

381
00:12:55,119 --> 00:12:57,275
and some keep much of their savings

382
00:12:57,275 --> 00:12:59,000
intact well into retirement,

383
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,279
often out of caution and uncertainty.

384
00:13:01,629 --> 00:13:03,017
Caution can be wise,

385
00:13:03,017 --> 00:13:05,309
but underspending has a cost too.

386
00:13:05,489 --> 00:13:07,846
Money saved for decades might never be

387
00:13:07,846 --> 00:13:10,328
used for the experiences and security it

388
00:13:10,328 --> 00:13:11,631
was meant to provide.

389
00:13:11,981 --> 00:13:13,561
A clear withdrawal plan,

390
00:13:13,561 --> 00:13:15,338
like the four percent rule,

391
00:13:15,338 --> 00:13:17,577
can actually make spending easier.

392
00:13:17,757 --> 00:13:19,543
It gives you permission to spend a

393
00:13:19,543 --> 00:13:20,961
set amount with confidence.

394
00:13:21,311 --> 00:13:22,112
Try this.

395
00:13:22,292 --> 00:13:23,946
If you're retired or close,

396
00:13:23,946 --> 00:13:26,274
write down what a safe yearly spending

397
00:13:26,274 --> 00:13:27,867
amount looks like for you,

398
00:13:27,867 --> 00:13:30,256
and give yourself permission to use it.

399
00:13:30,606 --> 00:13:31,860
Lesson number ten.

400
00:13:32,040 --> 00:13:34,687
Lifestyle creep moves the finish line.

401
00:13:35,037 --> 00:13:36,184
As income grows,

402
00:13:36,184 --> 00:13:38,264
spending often grows with it.

403
00:13:38,444 --> 00:13:40,400
Bigger house, nicer car,

404
00:13:40,400 --> 00:13:42,438
more expensive vacations.

405
00:13:42,618 --> 00:13:44,148
Each upgrade feels small,

406
00:13:44,148 --> 00:13:46,902
but together they raise your yearly spending.

407
00:13:47,252 --> 00:13:49,592
Because of the times twenty five rule,

408
00:13:49,592 --> 00:13:52,486
every permanent ten thousand dollar increase in

409
00:13:52,486 --> 00:13:55,257
yearly spending raises your freedom number by

410
00:13:55,257 --> 00:13:57,782
about two hundred fifty thousand dollars.

411
00:13:57,962 --> 00:14:00,725
Lifestyle creep doesn't just reduce what you

412
00:14:00,725 --> 00:14:01,039
save.

413
00:14:01,219 --> 00:14:03,448
It moves the finish line further away.

414
00:14:03,798 --> 00:14:05,574
The opposite is also true.

415
00:14:05,754 --> 00:14:08,924
Reducing yearly spending by ten thousand dollars

416
00:14:08,924 --> 00:14:11,433
lowers the target by about two hundred

417
00:14:11,433 --> 00:14:13,876
fifty thousand, and frees up money to

418
00:14:13,876 --> 00:14:14,206
save.

419
00:14:14,386 --> 00:14:15,617
It's a double benefit.

420
00:14:15,967 --> 00:14:18,080
This doesn't mean never upgrading your life.

421
00:14:18,260 --> 00:14:20,826
It means making upgrades deliberately,

422
00:14:20,826 --> 00:14:23,257
knowing what each one costs in years

423
00:14:23,257 --> 00:14:23,798
of work.

424
00:14:24,148 --> 00:14:24,972
Try this.

425
00:14:25,152 --> 00:14:27,493
Before any big recurring expense,

426
00:14:27,493 --> 00:14:30,330
multiply its yearly cost by twenty five.

427
00:14:30,510 --> 00:14:32,126
That's what it adds to your freedom

428
00:14:32,126 --> 00:14:32,449
number.

429
00:14:32,799 --> 00:14:33,855
Quick pause.

430
00:14:34,035 --> 00:14:36,781
If knowing your freedom number sounds useful,

431
00:14:36,781 --> 00:14:39,039
please subscribe to Ryan Rover Money.

432
00:14:39,219 --> 00:14:41,010
It's free, and it helps us keep

433
00:14:41,010 --> 00:14:41,762
making these.

434
00:14:41,942 --> 00:14:43,718
Now, lesson eleven.

435
00:14:44,068 --> 00:14:45,531
Lesson number eleven.

436
00:14:45,711 --> 00:14:46,930
One more year syndrome.

437
00:14:47,280 --> 00:14:49,515
Many people reach their number and still

438
00:14:49,515 --> 00:14:50,577
don't stop working.

439
00:14:50,757 --> 00:14:52,874
They think, just one more year,

440
00:14:52,874 --> 00:14:53,625
to be safe.

441
00:14:53,805 --> 00:14:54,501
Then another.

442
00:14:54,681 --> 00:14:55,424
And another.

443
00:14:55,604 --> 00:14:58,018
The finish line keeps moving because fear

444
00:14:58,018 --> 00:14:58,959
keeps moving it.

445
00:14:59,310 --> 00:15:01,606
Some extra cushion can be smart,

446
00:15:01,606 --> 00:15:04,047
especially with uncertain markets.

447
00:15:04,227 --> 00:15:06,015
But years are the one resource you

448
00:15:06,015 --> 00:15:06,804
can't get back.

449
00:15:06,984 --> 00:15:10,105
Working years longer than necessary trades healthy,

450
00:15:10,105 --> 00:15:12,309
active years for money you may never

451
00:15:12,309 --> 00:15:12,615
need.

452
00:15:12,965 --> 00:15:14,538
A plan with flexibility,

453
00:15:14,538 --> 00:15:16,964
like being willing to cut spending in

454
00:15:16,964 --> 00:15:19,063
bad years or earn some part time

455
00:15:19,063 --> 00:15:21,554
income, can give you the confidence to

456
00:15:21,554 --> 00:15:24,505
stop when you've reached a reasonable target.

457
00:15:24,855 --> 00:15:26,770
It also helps to think about what

458
00:15:26,770 --> 00:15:27,873
you're retiring to,

459
00:15:27,873 --> 00:15:28,686
not just from.

460
00:15:28,866 --> 00:15:31,124
People with clear plans for their time

461
00:15:31,124 --> 00:15:33,085
often feel more ready to make the

462
00:15:33,085 --> 00:15:33,382
leap.

463
00:15:33,732 --> 00:15:34,486
Try this.

464
00:15:34,666 --> 00:15:36,552
Write down what would make you feel

465
00:15:36,552 --> 00:15:38,115
ready to stop full time work.

466
00:15:38,295 --> 00:15:39,789
A number, a buffer,

467
00:15:39,789 --> 00:15:41,441
a plan for your days.

468
00:15:41,621 --> 00:15:42,863
Then hold yourself to it.

469
00:15:43,213 --> 00:15:44,629
Lesson number twelve.

470
00:15:44,809 --> 00:15:47,506
Financial freedom doesn't have to mean stopping

471
00:15:47,506 --> 00:15:47,793
work.

472
00:15:48,143 --> 00:15:51,303
Many people who reach financial independence keep

473
00:15:51,303 --> 00:15:53,367
working, but on their own terms.

474
00:15:53,547 --> 00:15:55,947
Part time, a passion project,

475
00:15:55,947 --> 00:15:58,596
a lower stress job or starting a

476
00:15:58,596 --> 00:15:59,341
business.

477
00:15:59,521 --> 00:16:01,030
The freedom is in the choice.

478
00:16:01,380 --> 00:16:04,654
Even partial financial independence is powerful.

479
00:16:04,834 --> 00:16:07,080
If your savings could cover half your

480
00:16:07,080 --> 00:16:09,448
spending, you could take a lower paying

481
00:16:09,448 --> 00:16:10,237
job you love,

482
00:16:10,237 --> 00:16:11,451
or work fewer hours.

483
00:16:11,802 --> 00:16:13,857
Some people use the idea of coast

484
00:16:13,857 --> 00:16:16,535
FIRE, where they've saved enough that their

485
00:16:16,535 --> 00:16:19,213
investments could grow to their full number

486
00:16:19,213 --> 00:16:21,082
by traditional retirement age,

487
00:16:21,082 --> 00:16:23,075
even without more contributions.

488
00:16:23,255 --> 00:16:25,342
From there, they only need to cover

489
00:16:25,342 --> 00:16:26,355
current expenses.

490
00:16:26,705 --> 00:16:28,987
Earning even a small income in early

491
00:16:28,987 --> 00:16:31,965
retirement can dramatically reduce how much you

492
00:16:31,965 --> 00:16:33,043
need to withdraw,

493
00:16:33,043 --> 00:16:35,261
making your money last much longer.

494
00:16:35,611 --> 00:16:36,377
Try this.

495
00:16:36,557 --> 00:16:39,149
Calculate what percentage of your spending your

496
00:16:39,149 --> 00:16:41,631
savings could cover today using four percent.

497
00:16:41,811 --> 00:16:43,506
That's your current level of freedom.

498
00:16:43,856 --> 00:16:45,423
Lesson number thirteen.

499
00:16:45,603 --> 00:16:47,170
Define enough for yourself.

500
00:16:47,521 --> 00:16:49,802
Some people chase five or ten million

501
00:16:49,802 --> 00:16:52,391
because that's what wealthy people seem to

502
00:16:52,391 --> 00:16:52,699
have.

503
00:16:52,879 --> 00:16:55,221
But your freedom number depends only on

504
00:16:55,221 --> 00:16:56,302
your own spending,

505
00:16:56,302 --> 00:16:57,383
not anyone else's.

506
00:16:57,734 --> 00:16:59,815
Morgan Housel writes that one of the

507
00:16:59,815 --> 00:17:02,590
hardest financial skills is getting the goalpost

508
00:17:02,590 --> 00:17:03,457
to stop moving.

509
00:17:03,637 --> 00:17:05,490
Without a sense of enough,

510
00:17:05,490 --> 00:17:07,771
no amount ever feels sufficient.

511
00:17:08,121 --> 00:17:10,158
A clear number tied to a lifestyle

512
00:17:10,158 --> 00:17:12,196
you actually want can bring peace.

513
00:17:12,376 --> 00:17:13,999
You know what you're working toward,

514
00:17:13,999 --> 00:17:15,487
and you know when you've arrived.

515
00:17:15,837 --> 00:17:17,509
That doesn't mean you can't want more.

516
00:17:17,689 --> 00:17:19,409
It means the more is a choice,

517
00:17:19,409 --> 00:17:21,531
not a requirement for feeling secure.

518
00:17:21,882 --> 00:17:22,706
Try this.

519
00:17:22,886 --> 00:17:25,790
Describe your ideal ordinary week in retirement,

520
00:17:25,790 --> 00:17:27,484
then estimate what it costs.

521
00:17:27,664 --> 00:17:29,556
That's the foundation of your enough.

522
00:17:29,906 --> 00:17:31,403
Lesson number fourteen.

523
00:17:31,583 --> 00:17:33,859
Flexible plans beat rigid ones.

524
00:17:34,209 --> 00:17:36,922
The original four percent rule assumes you

525
00:17:36,922 --> 00:17:39,764
raise withdrawals with inflation every year,

526
00:17:39,764 --> 00:17:40,734
no matter what.

527
00:17:40,914 --> 00:17:43,491
Real people don't usually behave that rigidly.

528
00:17:43,671 --> 00:17:45,645
They cut back in bad years and

529
00:17:45,645 --> 00:17:47,224
spend more in good ones.

530
00:17:47,574 --> 00:17:50,493
Research on flexible withdrawal strategies,

531
00:17:50,493 --> 00:17:53,887
such as guardrail approaches developed by planners

532
00:17:53,887 --> 00:17:56,331
Jonathan Guyton and William Klinger,

533
00:17:56,331 --> 00:17:59,318
suggests that being willing to trim spending

534
00:17:59,318 --> 00:18:02,441
after poor markets can support higher starting

535
00:18:02,441 --> 00:18:03,595
withdrawal rates.

536
00:18:03,946 --> 00:18:06,152
Flexibility reduces anxiety.

537
00:18:06,332 --> 00:18:08,309
If you know you'd cut travel for

538
00:18:08,309 --> 00:18:10,101
a year or two during a crash,

539
00:18:10,101 --> 00:18:11,955
a bad market feels less like a

540
00:18:11,955 --> 00:18:13,994
threat to your entire retirement.

541
00:18:14,344 --> 00:18:17,478
Separating spending into needs and wants helps.

542
00:18:17,658 --> 00:18:20,584
Cover essential needs with stable income and

543
00:18:20,584 --> 00:18:23,044
safer assets, and fund wants from the

544
00:18:23,044 --> 00:18:24,241
riskier portfolio.

545
00:18:24,591 --> 00:18:25,485
Try this.

546
00:18:25,665 --> 00:18:28,426
List which retirement expenses you could reduce

547
00:18:28,426 --> 00:18:30,483
for a year if markets fell sharply.

548
00:18:30,663 --> 00:18:32,474
That list is your safety valve.

549
00:18:32,824 --> 00:18:33,857
That's angle two.

550
00:18:34,037 --> 00:18:35,764
The number makes it real,

551
00:18:35,764 --> 00:18:36,938
fear of spending,

552
00:18:36,938 --> 00:18:39,079
lifestyle creep, one more year,

553
00:18:39,079 --> 00:18:41,289
freedom without full retirement,

554
00:18:41,289 --> 00:18:43,499
defining enough and flexibility.

555
00:18:43,679 --> 00:18:44,898
Now angle three.

556
00:18:45,078 --> 00:18:45,868
The playbook.

557
00:18:46,218 --> 00:18:48,509
Let's turn the four percent rule into

558
00:18:48,509 --> 00:18:49,561
a practical plan.

559
00:18:49,741 --> 00:18:51,993
Six moves to calculate your number,

560
00:18:51,993 --> 00:18:53,666
reach it and make it last.

561
00:18:54,016 --> 00:18:55,374
Move number fifteen.

562
00:18:55,554 --> 00:18:57,029
Track your real spending.

563
00:18:57,379 --> 00:18:59,351
Your freedom number is only as good

564
00:18:59,351 --> 00:19:00,815
as your spending estimate.

565
00:19:00,995 --> 00:19:03,972
Many people underestimate what they spend by

566
00:19:03,972 --> 00:19:06,949
a lot, because small purchases and irregular

567
00:19:06,949 --> 00:19:08,031
expenses add up.

568
00:19:08,381 --> 00:19:10,447
Look at at least three to six

569
00:19:10,447 --> 00:19:13,439
months of bank and credit card statements,

570
00:19:13,439 --> 00:19:16,004
plus yearly expenses like insurance,

571
00:19:16,004 --> 00:19:18,284
gifts, car repairs and holidays.

