your savings are in pre tax accounts,

858
00:28:57,797 --> 00:29:00,465
since withdrawals there are usually taxed.

859
00:29:00,645 --> 00:29:01,830
That's your freedom number.

860
00:29:02,180 --> 00:29:03,155
Step five.

861
00:29:03,335 --> 00:29:05,406
Compare your freedom number with what you

862
00:29:05,406 --> 00:29:07,073
have today, and work out the gap.

863
00:29:07,253 --> 00:29:09,461
Then decide how you'll close it,

864
00:29:09,461 --> 00:29:10,496
by saving more,

865
00:29:10,496 --> 00:29:12,427
earning more, spending less,

866
00:29:12,427 --> 00:29:15,186
working a little longer or combining all

867
00:29:15,186 --> 00:29:15,531
four.

868
00:29:15,711 --> 00:29:17,798
Write the plan next to the number,

869
00:29:17,798 --> 00:29:19,578
and revisit both once a year.

870
00:29:19,928 --> 00:29:21,292
Imagine two retirees,

871
00:29:21,292 --> 00:29:23,240
each with one million dollars,

872
00:29:23,240 --> 00:29:26,358
each planning to withdraw forty thousand dollars

873
00:29:26,358 --> 00:29:26,813
a year.

874
00:29:26,993 --> 00:29:29,261
In their second year of retirement,

875
00:29:29,261 --> 00:29:31,335
the market drops thirty percent.

876
00:29:31,515 --> 00:29:33,696
Both portfolios fall sharply,

877
00:29:33,696 --> 00:29:35,276
and both feel scared.

878
00:29:35,626 --> 00:29:37,356
The first retiree panics,

879
00:29:37,356 --> 00:29:40,332
sells all their stocks and moves everything

880
00:29:40,332 --> 00:29:40,885
to cash.

881
00:29:41,065 --> 00:29:43,176
When markets recover over the next few

882
00:29:43,176 --> 00:29:44,676
years, their money doesn't.

883
00:29:44,856 --> 00:29:47,614
They keep withdrawing forty thousand a year

884
00:29:47,614 --> 00:29:49,025
from a shrinking pile,

885
00:29:49,025 --> 00:29:50,886
and their plan is now at real

886
00:29:50,886 --> 00:29:51,206
risk.

887
00:29:51,557 --> 00:29:53,575
The second retiree had a two year

888
00:29:53,575 --> 00:29:54,308
cash buffer.

889
00:29:54,488 --> 00:29:55,626
They spend from it,

890
00:29:55,626 --> 00:29:57,542
trim travel for a year and leave

891
00:29:57,542 --> 00:29:58,680
their stocks alone.

892
00:29:58,860 --> 00:30:00,435
When markets recover,

893
00:30:00,435 --> 00:30:02,610
their portfolio recovers too.

894
00:30:02,790 --> 00:30:05,396
Same crash, very different outcomes,

895
00:30:05,396 --> 00:30:07,568
because one had a plan for bad

896
00:30:07,568 --> 00:30:08,002
years.

897
00:30:08,353 --> 00:30:10,535
The four percent rule didn't save the

898
00:30:10,535 --> 00:30:12,010
second retiree by itself.

899
00:30:12,190 --> 00:30:14,370
What saved them was the system around

900
00:30:14,370 --> 00:30:14,547
it.

901
00:30:14,727 --> 00:30:15,988
A realistic number,

902
00:30:15,988 --> 00:30:17,847
a cash buffer for bad years,

903
00:30:17,847 --> 00:30:20,568
a willingness to adjust spending a little

904
00:30:20,568 --> 00:30:22,958
and the discipline to stay invested.

905
00:30:23,138 --> 00:30:24,973
The rule tells you where to aim.

906
00:30:25,153 --> 00:30:27,158
The system is what gets you through

907
00:30:27,158 --> 00:30:27,788
the storms.

908
00:30:28,138 --> 00:30:29,055
Question one.

909
00:30:29,235 --> 00:30:31,046
Is four percent too high now?

910
00:30:31,226 --> 00:30:34,727
Some researchers suggest slightly lower starting rates

911
00:30:34,727 --> 00:30:37,774
for long retirements or lower expected returns.

912
00:30:37,954 --> 00:30:40,799
Others think four percent remains reasonable.

913
00:30:40,979 --> 00:30:42,907
Using three and a half to four

914
00:30:42,907 --> 00:30:45,672
percent with flexibility is a common middle

915
00:30:45,672 --> 00:30:46,122
ground.

916
00:30:46,472 --> 00:30:47,354
Question two.

917
00:30:47,534 --> 00:30:49,833
Does the rule work for early retirement?

918
00:30:50,013 --> 00:30:51,735
It can be a starting point,

919
00:30:51,735 --> 00:30:54,605
but longer retirements usually call for lower

920
00:30:54,605 --> 00:30:57,284
withdrawal rates, often three to three and

921
00:30:57,284 --> 00:30:58,240
a half percent,

922
00:30:58,240 --> 00:30:59,580
and more flexibility.

923
00:30:59,930 --> 00:31:00,975
Question three.

924
00:31:01,155 --> 00:31:02,351
What if I have a pension?

925
00:31:02,531 --> 00:31:04,891
A pension, like Social Security,

926
00:31:04,891 --> 00:31:07,767
reduces how much you need from savings.

927
00:31:07,947 --> 00:31:10,960
Subtract it from your spending before multiplying

928
00:31:10,960 --> 00:31:11,882
by twenty five.

929
00:31:12,233 --> 00:31:13,138
Question four.

930
00:31:13,318 --> 00:31:15,536
Should I include my home's value?

931
00:31:15,716 --> 00:31:18,253
Usually not, unless you plan to sell

932
00:31:18,253 --> 00:31:20,579
or downsize and use the money for

933
00:31:20,579 --> 00:31:21,707
living expenses.

934
00:31:21,887 --> 00:31:23,611
Your home provides shelter,

935
00:31:23,611 --> 00:31:24,824
not spending money.

936
00:31:25,174 --> 00:31:26,230
Question five.

937
00:31:26,410 --> 00:31:28,477
What about long term care costs?

938
00:31:28,657 --> 00:31:31,229
Long term care can be very expensive,

939
00:31:31,229 --> 00:31:33,802
and many retirees will need some help

940
00:31:33,802 --> 00:31:34,775
later in life.

941
00:31:34,955 --> 00:31:37,292
Some people plan for it with long

942
00:31:37,292 --> 00:31:38,709
term care insurance,

943
00:31:38,709 --> 00:31:41,400
others set aside extra savings or plan

944
00:31:41,400 --> 00:31:42,746
to use home equity.

945
00:31:42,926 --> 00:31:45,102
It's worth discussing with a planner,

946
00:31:45,102 --> 00:31:46,748
because it can be one of the

947
00:31:46,748 --> 00:31:48,336
biggest late life expenses.

948
00:31:48,686 --> 00:31:49,684
Question six.

949
00:31:49,864 --> 00:31:51,721
Can I retire with less than twenty

950
00:31:51,721 --> 00:31:52,813
five times spending?

951
00:31:52,993 --> 00:31:55,292
Possibly, with more flexibility.

952
00:31:55,472 --> 00:31:56,754
Part time income,

953
00:31:56,754 --> 00:31:58,640
delaying Social Security,

954
00:31:58,640 --> 00:32:01,355
a willingness to cut spending in bad

955
00:32:01,355 --> 00:32:03,769
years, or moving to a lower cost

956
00:32:03,769 --> 00:32:06,333
area can all make a smaller number

957
00:32:06,333 --> 00:32:06,710
work.

958
00:32:06,890 --> 00:32:08,425
The more flexible you are,

959
00:32:08,425 --> 00:32:09,723
the less you may need.

960
00:32:10,073 --> 00:32:10,978
Mistake one.

961
00:32:11,158 --> 00:32:13,713
Using income instead of spending.

962
00:32:13,893 --> 00:32:16,026
Your freedom number is built from what

963
00:32:16,026 --> 00:32:17,654
you spend, not what you earn.

964
00:32:17,834 --> 00:32:20,238
Using income can make your target far

965
00:32:20,238 --> 00:32:21,018
too high or,

966
00:32:21,018 --> 00:32:23,161
if you spend more than you think,

967
00:32:23,161 --> 00:32:23,941
far too low.

968
00:32:24,291 --> 00:32:25,371
Mistake two.

969
00:32:25,551 --> 00:32:27,803
Forgetting health care before Medicare.

970
00:32:27,983 --> 00:32:30,665
People who retire before sixty five need

971
00:32:30,665 --> 00:32:33,279
health insurance until Medicare starts,

972
00:32:33,279 --> 00:32:35,425
and premiums can be significant.

973
00:32:35,605 --> 00:32:38,310
Build that cost into your spending estimate.

974
00:32:38,660 --> 00:32:39,716
Mistake three.

975
00:32:39,896 --> 00:32:42,218
Treating four percent as guaranteed.

976
00:32:42,398 --> 00:32:44,233
It's a historical rule of thumb.

977
00:32:44,413 --> 00:32:47,463
Markets, inflation and your lifespan can differ

978
00:32:47,463 --> 00:32:48,372
from the past.

979
00:32:48,552 --> 00:32:50,687
Build in a margin of safety and

980
00:32:50,687 --> 00:32:51,652
stay flexible.

981
00:32:52,002 --> 00:32:52,977
Mistake four.

982
00:32:53,157 --> 00:32:54,434
Ignoring taxes.

983
00:32:54,614 --> 00:32:57,817
Withdrawals from pre tax accounts are usually

984
00:32:57,817 --> 00:32:58,956
taxed as income.

985
00:32:59,136 --> 00:33:01,116
If you need forty thousand dollars to

986
00:33:01,116 --> 00:33:03,095
spend, you may need to withdraw more.

987
00:33:03,275 --> 00:33:04,889
Plan for the tax bill.

988
00:33:05,239 --> 00:33:06,110
Quick recap.

989
00:33:06,290 --> 00:33:08,008
Angle one, the numbers.

990
00:33:08,188 --> 00:33:10,172
Where the rule came from,

991
00:33:10,172 --> 00:33:12,315
the times twenty five rule,

992
00:33:12,315 --> 00:33:14,616
sequence risk, no guarantees,

993
00:33:14,616 --> 00:33:16,918
time horizon, investment mix,

994
00:33:16,918 --> 00:33:18,823
and inflation and taxes.

995
00:33:19,173 --> 00:33:21,390
Angle two, the psychology.

996
00:33:21,570 --> 00:33:23,254
A number makes it real,

997
00:33:23,254 --> 00:33:24,498
fear of spending,

998
00:33:24,498 --> 00:33:26,767
lifestyle creep, one more year,

999
00:33:26,767 --> 00:33:29,108
freedom without full retirement,

1000
00:33:29,108 --> 00:33:31,450
defining enough and flexibility.

1001
00:33:31,801 --> 00:33:33,542
Angle three, the playbook.

1002
00:33:33,722 --> 00:33:36,423
Track spending, calculate your number,

1003
00:33:36,423 --> 00:33:38,271
factor in Social Security,

1004
00:33:38,271 --> 00:33:39,266
close the gap,

1005
00:33:39,266 --> 00:33:42,109
protect the early years and review every

1006
00:33:42,109 --> 00:33:42,465
year.

1007
00:33:42,815 --> 00:33:44,870
Your freedom number isn't a fantasy.

1008
00:33:45,050 --> 00:33:47,764
It's your yearly spending times twenty five,

1009
00:33:47,764 --> 00:33:49,183
adjusted for your life.

1010
00:33:49,363 --> 00:33:50,204
Once you know it,

1011
00:33:50,204 --> 00:33:51,588
you can build a real plan to

1012
00:33:51,588 --> 00:33:52,033
reach it.

1013
00:33:52,213 --> 00:33:53,803
If you want my full step by

1014
00:33:53,803 --> 00:33:55,628
step plan, it's in my playbook,

1015
00:33:55,628 --> 00:33:57,159
linked in the description.

1016
00:33:57,339 --> 00:33:58,645
Tell me in the comments,

1017
00:33:58,645 --> 00:34:00,114
what's your freedom number?

1018
00:34:00,294 --> 00:34:01,478
I'm Ryan Rover.

1019
00:34:01,658 --> 00:34:02,877
See you in the next one.
